WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.374 --> 00:00:08.154
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.154 --> 00:00:10.054
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.054 --> 00:00:12.994
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.014 --> 00:00:14.744
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.744 --> 00:00:17.364
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.374 --> 00:00:19.444
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.444 --> 00:00:21.234
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:24.584
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we

00:00:24.584 --> 00:00:26.174
are going to be talking about a

00:00:26.174 --> 00:00:29.164
man called Michael Calvert, or as

00:00:29.164 --> 00:00:31.624
he was better known, "Mad Mike".

00:00:32.054 --> 00:00:34.854
He was one of the most decorated British

00:00:34.864 --> 00:00:37.354
soldiers of the Second World War, a

00:00:37.354 --> 00:00:40.034
man whose ideas still shape the most

00:00:40.034 --> 00:00:42.064
famous regiment in the British Army.

00:00:42.604 --> 00:00:44.984
And his story ends with one of

00:00:44.994 --> 00:00:46.904
the saddest falls from grace

00:00:47.134 --> 00:00:48.804
in British military history.

00:00:49.144 --> 00:00:51.114
So let's not waste a minute

00:00:51.464 --> 00:00:53.364
and get right into it.

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:57.857
In 1997, a set of military

00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:00.267
medals was put up for sale.

00:01:00.917 --> 00:01:02.537
There was nothing particularly

00:01:02.727 --> 00:01:03.927
unusual about that.

00:01:04.227 --> 00:01:06.507
Medals are bought and sold all

00:01:06.507 --> 00:01:08.877
the time, usually long after

00:01:08.877 --> 00:01:10.617
the original owner has died.

00:01:11.097 --> 00:01:15.067
But these medals belonged to a living man.

00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:18.517
He was 84 years old, he was living in

00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:21.237
south-west London, and he was selling

00:01:21.237 --> 00:01:23.357
them because he needed the money.

00:01:24.437 --> 00:01:26.987
Among them were two Distinguished

00:01:26.997 --> 00:01:29.397
Service Orders, which is one of the

00:01:29.407 --> 00:01:31.537
most distinguished medals you can get.

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:34.267
The man selling them was

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:35.967
called Michael Calvert.

00:01:36.227 --> 00:01:38.607
And fifty years earlier, he had

00:01:38.607 --> 00:01:40.377
been one of the most celebrated

00:01:40.387 --> 00:01:42.337
soldiers in the British Army.

00:01:43.177 --> 00:01:45.477
He had led troops behind Japanese

00:01:45.487 --> 00:01:47.597
lines in the jungles of Burma.

00:01:47.897 --> 00:01:50.357
He had been recommended for the Victoria

00:01:50.357 --> 00:01:52.357
Cross, Britain's highest military

00:01:52.357 --> 00:01:54.967
honour, on three separate occasions.

00:01:55.297 --> 00:01:58.517
He had commanded the SAS, and later,

00:01:58.717 --> 00:02:00.727
he had brought it back from the dead.

00:02:01.227 --> 00:02:03.707
So, you might be thinking, how does a

00:02:03.707 --> 00:02:07.027
man like that end up poor, and forgotten,

00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:10.387
selling his own medals to pay his bills?

00:02:11.347 --> 00:02:13.267
The answer involves one of the

00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:15.427
most remarkable military careers

00:02:15.637 --> 00:02:17.497
of the twentieth century, and

00:02:17.727 --> 00:02:20.307
a single trial in Germany that

00:02:20.327 --> 00:02:22.497
destroyed it almost overnight.

00:02:23.517 --> 00:02:25.427
Let's start at the beginning.

00:02:25.977 --> 00:02:27.707
Michael Calvert was born

00:02:27.717 --> 00:02:31.037
in India in March of 1913.

00:02:31.807 --> 00:02:34.637
His father was a senior official in the

00:02:34.657 --> 00:02:37.217
British administration there, and like

00:02:37.217 --> 00:02:40.187
many sons of empire, young Michael was

00:02:40.187 --> 00:02:42.547
sent back to England for his education.

00:02:43.427 --> 00:02:45.527
He went on to the Royal Military

00:02:45.527 --> 00:02:48.207
Academy and joined the Royal Engineers.

00:02:48.647 --> 00:02:50.607
The Royal Engineers are the part

00:02:50.607 --> 00:02:52.347
of the army that builds things:

00:02:52.606 --> 00:02:54.806
bridges, roads and defences.

00:02:55.337 --> 00:02:57.687
They are also the part of the army that

00:02:57.707 --> 00:03:00.447
blows things up, and it was this second

00:03:00.447 --> 00:03:02.947
skill that Calvert would make his own.

00:03:03.997 --> 00:03:06.707
He was also a natural athlete.

00:03:06.837 --> 00:03:09.167
He boxed for the Army, becoming

00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:10.857
its middleweight champion, and

00:03:11.097 --> 00:03:12.397
he was also a good swimmer.

00:03:12.967 --> 00:03:15.557
He was clever, too, and was sent

00:03:15.577 --> 00:03:17.517
by the army to Cambridge, to

00:03:17.517 --> 00:03:19.197
study Mechanical Engineering.

00:03:19.917 --> 00:03:23.247
In 1934, he was posted to Hong

00:03:23.247 --> 00:03:25.657
Kong, where he learned Cantonese.

00:03:26.267 --> 00:03:29.937
And in 1937, he was present during the

00:03:29.947 --> 00:03:32.465
Japanese assault on Shanghai, shortly

00:03:32.544 --> 00:03:35.104
before the infamous massacre  at Nanjing,

00:03:35.475 --> 00:03:37.965
witnessing one of the most modern and

00:03:37.975 --> 00:03:40.265
brutal armies in the world at work.

00:03:41.215 --> 00:03:42.785
This was something of an

00:03:42.865 --> 00:03:44.845
eye-opener for the young officer.

00:03:45.605 --> 00:03:48.215
At the time, the general view

00:03:48.225 --> 00:03:49.905
in the British military was that

00:03:49.905 --> 00:03:52.525
Japan was not a serious threat.

00:03:52.855 --> 00:03:55.625
Its soldiers were thought to be poorly

00:03:55.625 --> 00:03:58.805
equipped and no match for European troops.

00:03:59.675 --> 00:04:02.025
Calvert had seen them fight.

00:04:02.165 --> 00:04:04.845
He knew this view was dangerously

00:04:04.845 --> 00:04:07.365
wrong, and within a few years,

00:04:07.605 --> 00:04:09.115
he would be proved right.

00:04:09.855 --> 00:04:13.095
Now, when the Second World War broke out,

00:04:13.355 --> 00:04:16.225
Calvert had a brief and frustrating time

00:04:16.475 --> 00:04:19.665
in the failed Norwegian campaign of 1940.

00:04:20.145 --> 00:04:22.785
But the army had noticed his talent

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:25.325
for demolitions and irregular warfare,

00:04:25.745 --> 00:04:29.415
and in 1941 he was sent east again,

00:04:29.805 --> 00:04:31.515
to run something called the Bush

00:04:31.535 --> 00:04:34.254
Warfare School in Burma, in modern day

00:04:34.325 --> 00:04:36.975
Myanmar, but then a British colony.

00:04:38.185 --> 00:04:41.655
His job was to train soldiers in guerrilla

00:04:41.665 --> 00:04:44.765
warfare: how to operate in small groups

00:04:44.885 --> 00:04:47.815
behind enemy lines, how to ambush, how to

00:04:48.005 --> 00:04:50.925
sabotage, how to survive in the jungle.

00:04:51.785 --> 00:04:55.775
So when the Japanese invaded Burma in

00:04:55.855 --> 00:04:59.335
early 1942, Michael Calvert was one

00:04:59.375 --> 00:05:01.925
of the few men in the entire British

00:05:01.925 --> 00:05:04.435
Army who knew how to fight back.

00:05:05.535 --> 00:05:07.085
There was very little he could

00:05:07.095 --> 00:05:09.295
do, though, because the campaign

00:05:09.305 --> 00:05:11.715
was a disaster from the start.

00:05:12.725 --> 00:05:14.825
The Japanese soldiers who crossed

00:05:14.835 --> 00:05:18.249
into Burma in January 1942 were

00:05:18.295 --> 00:05:20.775
hardened veterans, with years of

00:05:20.785 --> 00:05:22.555
fighting in China behind them.

00:05:23.035 --> 00:05:25.315
They were trained for the jungle, they

00:05:25.485 --> 00:05:28.125
travelled light, and they moved fast.

00:05:29.305 --> 00:05:31.295
The British defence, on the other

00:05:31.295 --> 00:05:33.865
hand, was built around roads.

00:05:34.765 --> 00:05:36.765
The thinking was that the thick

00:05:36.845 --> 00:05:40.135
Burmese jungle was impassable, so the

00:05:40.145 --> 00:05:42.825
jungle was left largely unguarded.

00:05:43.725 --> 00:05:46.515
The Japanese simply walked through it,

00:05:46.695 --> 00:05:48.575
slipped around the British positions,

00:05:48.835 --> 00:05:50.805
and set up roadblocks behind them.

00:05:51.565 --> 00:05:54.065
Again and again, British units

00:05:54.265 --> 00:05:56.585
discovered that the enemy was already

00:05:56.585 --> 00:05:58.775
behind them, cutting off their escape.

00:05:59.955 --> 00:06:02.715
And to make matters worse, many

00:06:02.745 --> 00:06:05.165
of the defending troops were raw

00:06:05.215 --> 00:06:07.465
recruits who had never seen battle.

00:06:08.505 --> 00:06:11.025
When the capital and main port of

00:06:11.025 --> 00:06:12.995
Rangoon fell just a couple of months

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:15.885
later, there was no longer any way to

00:06:15.895 --> 00:06:18.345
bring in supplies or reinforcements.

00:06:19.685 --> 00:06:21.675
What followed was the longest

00:06:21.695 --> 00:06:24.075
retreat in British military history.

00:06:24.825 --> 00:06:27.809
The army fell back around 1,500

00:06:27.809 --> 00:06:29.575
kilometres, through jungle,

00:06:29.935 --> 00:06:32.625
mountains and monsoon rains, all

00:06:32.645 --> 00:06:34.835
the way to India, with the Japanese

00:06:35.035 --> 00:06:37.055
close behind for most of the way.

00:06:38.135 --> 00:06:40.375
And it was during that chaotic

00:06:40.395 --> 00:06:42.655
retreat that Calvert met the

00:06:42.655 --> 00:06:44.655
man who would change his life.

00:06:45.624 --> 00:06:48.415
Orde Wingate was one of the strangest

00:06:48.455 --> 00:06:50.555
officers the British Army has ever

00:06:50.555 --> 00:06:53.055
produced, and that is saying something.

00:06:53.765 --> 00:06:57.555
He was intense, difficult, and completely

00:06:57.635 --> 00:06:59.845
indifferent to what anyone thought of him.

00:07:00.525 --> 00:07:03.125
He ate raw onions because he believed

00:07:03.125 --> 00:07:04.215
they were good for his health.

00:07:04.675 --> 00:07:06.435
He quoted the Old Testament

00:07:06.665 --> 00:07:07.925
in military briefings.

00:07:08.205 --> 00:07:10.715
He held meetings while completely naked,

00:07:11.015 --> 00:07:13.295
brushing his body with a rubber brush.

00:07:14.155 --> 00:07:16.515
Most officers thought he was mad.

00:07:17.195 --> 00:07:19.385
Calvert thought he was a genius.

00:07:20.195 --> 00:07:21.995
The two men became firm

00:07:21.995 --> 00:07:23.598
friends almost immediately.

00:07:24.738 --> 00:07:26.875
Wingate's big idea was this.

00:07:27.495 --> 00:07:29.915
Instead of facing the Japanese army

00:07:30.155 --> 00:07:33.105
head-on, Britain should send columns

00:07:33.125 --> 00:07:36.295
of specially trained troops deep behind

00:07:36.315 --> 00:07:38.415
enemy lines, marching through the

00:07:38.415 --> 00:07:41.320
jungle and supplied entirely by air.

00:07:41.915 --> 00:07:44.315
They would cut railways, destroy

00:07:44.315 --> 00:07:46.985
bridges, and attack the Japanese

00:07:47.145 --> 00:07:48.695
where they least expected it.

00:07:49.945 --> 00:07:52.835
The force became known as the Chindits,

00:07:52.975 --> 00:07:55.485
named after the chinthe, the mythical

00:07:55.485 --> 00:07:58.175
lion statues that guard Burmese temples.

00:07:59.565 --> 00:08:04.245
In February 1943, around 3,000 Chindits

00:08:04.465 --> 00:08:06.785
marched into Japanese-occupied Burma.

00:08:07.255 --> 00:08:09.185
Calvert commanded one of the columns.

00:08:10.005 --> 00:08:13.625
It was a brutal experience, and the jungle

00:08:13.625 --> 00:08:16.255
itself was as dangerous as the Japanese.

00:08:16.895 --> 00:08:20.045
It was dense, steaming hot, and

00:08:20.085 --> 00:08:22.775
crawling with leeches and mosquitoes.

00:08:23.385 --> 00:08:25.835
The men marched for weeks on end,

00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:28.215
carrying everything on their back and

00:08:28.215 --> 00:08:31.045
on mules, relying on supply drops from

00:08:31.045 --> 00:08:33.435
the air that didn't always arrive where

00:08:33.435 --> 00:08:35.505
they were meant to, or arrive at all.

00:08:36.395 --> 00:08:38.205
Malaria and dysentery

00:08:38.395 --> 00:08:39.985
spread through the columns.

00:08:40.205 --> 00:08:42.035
Rations ran short.

00:08:42.455 --> 00:08:44.815
And the men who were badly wounded

00:08:44.945 --> 00:08:47.575
or too sick to walk often had to

00:08:47.575 --> 00:08:49.485
be left behind, because there was

00:08:49.485 --> 00:08:51.335
simply no way to carry them out.

00:08:52.805 --> 00:08:54.725
The men were exhausted.

00:08:55.105 --> 00:08:57.855
Roughly a third of them never came back,

00:08:58.175 --> 00:09:01.185
and many of those who did were so broken

00:09:01.185 --> 00:09:03.565
by disease that they never fought again.

00:09:04.335 --> 00:09:06.905
But Calvert's column was the star

00:09:06.905 --> 00:09:09.155
of the operation, blowing up railway

00:09:09.155 --> 00:09:11.385
bridges and cutting off supply lines.

00:09:11.705 --> 00:09:14.415
And somehow, remarkably, getting

00:09:14.425 --> 00:09:16.495
back to India largely intact.

00:09:17.545 --> 00:09:18.585
He was awarded the

00:09:18.585 --> 00:09:20.165
Distinguished Service Order.

00:09:20.745 --> 00:09:23.655
And somewhere along the way, his men

00:09:23.865 --> 00:09:26.415
started calling him "Mad Mike", for

00:09:26.415 --> 00:09:28.485
his bravery, and sometimes "Dynamite

00:09:28.485 --> 00:09:30.055
Mike", for what he liked to do

00:09:30.235 --> 00:09:32.295
to Japanese bridges and railways.

00:09:33.645 --> 00:09:37.435
By early 1944, Wingate had convinced

00:09:37.445 --> 00:09:39.245
Winston Churchill and the Americans

00:09:39.465 --> 00:09:41.605
to back a far bigger operation.

00:09:42.435 --> 00:09:45.015
This time, an entire division would

00:09:45.025 --> 00:09:47.365
be flown into the heart of Burma,

00:09:47.715 --> 00:09:50.035
landing at night, in gliders,

00:09:50.245 --> 00:09:52.505
on jungle clearings hundreds of

00:09:52.505 --> 00:09:55.275
kilometres behind Japanese lines.

00:09:56.525 --> 00:09:59.315
Calvert, now a brigadier at the age of

00:09:59.315 --> 00:10:02.155
just 30, commanded the leading brigade.

00:10:03.425 --> 00:10:05.215
The landing zones had been given

00:10:05.215 --> 00:10:06.785
the names of famous streets:

00:10:07.125 --> 00:10:08.835
Piccadilly and Broadway.

00:10:09.675 --> 00:10:11.865
And on the evening of the operation,

00:10:12.055 --> 00:10:16.085
the 5th of March 1944, an aircraft

00:10:16.105 --> 00:10:18.085
brought back last-minute photographs

00:10:18.355 --> 00:10:21.025
showing that Piccadilly was blocked.

00:10:21.715 --> 00:10:23.225
Someone had covered the

00:10:23.225 --> 00:10:24.945
clearing with tree trunks.

00:10:26.125 --> 00:10:26.895
Was it a trap?

00:10:27.125 --> 00:10:29.265
Did the Japanese know they were coming?

00:10:30.065 --> 00:10:31.015
Nobody could say.

00:10:31.335 --> 00:10:33.635
Senior commanders hesitated, and the

00:10:33.635 --> 00:10:35.985
whole operation hung in the balance.

00:10:36.905 --> 00:10:38.895
Calvert did not hesitate.

00:10:39.455 --> 00:10:40.975
"I am prepared to take the whole of

00:10:40.975 --> 00:10:43.504
my brigade into Broadway," he said,

00:10:43.524 --> 00:10:45.545
"and we can do without Piccadilly."

00:10:46.135 --> 00:10:47.585
So they went.

00:10:48.165 --> 00:10:49.825
That night, Calvert and his

00:10:49.825 --> 00:10:52.045
men crash-landed in gliders

00:10:52.255 --> 00:10:54.065
onto a rough jungle clearing

00:10:54.335 --> 00:10:56.195
deep inside enemy territory.

00:10:56.815 --> 00:10:59.665
The gliders carried men, weapons,

00:10:59.845 --> 00:11:02.305
a few unlucky mules, and several

00:11:02.315 --> 00:11:04.035
small bulldozers, which the

00:11:04.035 --> 00:11:05.885
engineers used to carve a proper

00:11:05.935 --> 00:11:07.445
airstrip out of the clearing.

00:11:08.655 --> 00:11:11.185
The airstrip was ready within twenty-four

00:11:11.205 --> 00:11:14.125
hours, and from then on, transport

00:11:14.125 --> 00:11:16.335
planes landed night after night.

00:11:17.245 --> 00:11:20.215
Within a week, some 9,000 men

00:11:20.445 --> 00:11:23.535
and over 1,300 mules had been

00:11:23.535 --> 00:11:25.755
flown in behind Japanese lines.

00:11:27.135 --> 00:11:29.065
As it turned out, the tree trunks

00:11:29.085 --> 00:11:30.835
on Piccadilly had been left

00:11:30.835 --> 00:11:32.754
there by Burmese woodcutters.

00:11:33.135 --> 00:11:35.015
The Japanese had no idea

00:11:35.175 --> 00:11:36.195
the British were coming.

00:11:37.575 --> 00:11:39.175
What followed showed

00:11:39.275 --> 00:11:40.805
both sides of Calvert.

00:11:41.365 --> 00:11:43.165
There was the planner, who built

00:11:43.195 --> 00:11:45.225
a fortified base called White

00:11:45.225 --> 00:11:47.085
City, surrounded with barbed

00:11:47.095 --> 00:11:49.085
wire, mines and artillery.

00:11:50.245 --> 00:11:51.945
It got its name from the hundreds

00:11:51.945 --> 00:11:53.645
of white supply parachutes

00:11:53.805 --> 00:11:55.355
hanging in the trees around it.

00:11:56.485 --> 00:11:58.395
For weeks, the Japanese threw

00:11:58.395 --> 00:12:00.655
themselves at it in furious night

00:12:00.675 --> 00:12:03.125
attacks, and the defences Calvert

00:12:03.125 --> 00:12:05.264
had designed held every time.

00:12:06.424 --> 00:12:08.105
And then, there was the fighter.

00:12:09.405 --> 00:12:11.685
Early in the campaign, at a place called

00:12:11.715 --> 00:12:14.785
Pagoda Hill, Calvert found his men pinned

00:12:14.815 --> 00:12:17.635
down by Japanese troops on higher ground.

00:12:18.565 --> 00:12:21.005
His response was to get up and

00:12:21.165 --> 00:12:23.815
personally lead a bayonet charge

00:12:23.985 --> 00:12:25.915
up the slope, with officers and

00:12:25.915 --> 00:12:27.795
soldiers scrambling up beside him.

00:12:28.965 --> 00:12:32.145
The fighting at the top was hand-to-hand.

00:12:32.995 --> 00:12:35.195
Calvert later described it as an

00:12:35.205 --> 00:12:38.125
extraordinary scene, "everyone shooting,

00:12:38.355 --> 00:12:40.695
bayoneting, kicking at everyone else,

00:12:40.875 --> 00:12:42.745
rather like an officers' guest night."

00:12:44.045 --> 00:12:45.839
This, you understand, was a

00:12:46.177 --> 00:12:48.185
brigadier, a senior officer.

00:12:49.002 --> 00:12:51.205
Brigadiers are supposed to be

00:12:51.235 --> 00:12:52.895
several kilometres behind the

00:12:52.895 --> 00:12:55.025
fighting, sipping tea, looking at

00:12:55.025 --> 00:12:57.635
maps and radioing in instructions.

00:12:58.175 --> 00:13:01.425
That was never Calvert's style, and as you

00:13:01.425 --> 00:13:04.135
might imagine, his men loved him for it.

00:13:05.135 --> 00:13:09.455
Then, at the end of March 1944, disaster.

00:13:10.405 --> 00:13:13.235
Wingate, Calvert's hero, was killed

00:13:13.335 --> 00:13:15.605
when his plane crashed into a hillside.

00:13:16.175 --> 00:13:17.745
The Chindits had lost their

00:13:17.745 --> 00:13:19.295
leader and their protector.

00:13:20.445 --> 00:13:23.735
In May 1944, the Chindits were

00:13:23.775 --> 00:13:25.545
placed under the command of an

00:13:25.605 --> 00:13:28.105
American general, Joseph Stilwell,

00:13:28.414 --> 00:13:31.215
a man known as "Vinegar Joe".

00:13:32.255 --> 00:13:34.425
Stilwell had little time for the

00:13:34.425 --> 00:13:37.445
British and even less for the Chindits.

00:13:38.095 --> 00:13:39.735
Calvert's brigade was already

00:13:39.735 --> 00:13:41.765
worn down by months in the jungle.

00:13:42.705 --> 00:13:45.085
Then, it was ordered to capture the

00:13:45.085 --> 00:13:47.645
key Japanese stronghold of Mogaung.

00:13:48.815 --> 00:13:50.605
The monsoon had broken.

00:13:51.005 --> 00:13:52.105
The land was flooded.

00:13:52.395 --> 00:13:54.585
The men were exhausted, wet

00:13:54.585 --> 00:13:56.895
through, and riddled with malaria,

00:13:57.205 --> 00:13:59.465
dysentery and jungle sores.

00:13:59.875 --> 00:14:02.695
And Mogaung was defended by thousands

00:14:02.695 --> 00:14:04.905
of fresh Japanese troops with

00:14:04.925 --> 00:14:07.905
orders to hold it at all costs.

00:14:09.065 --> 00:14:11.725
Calvert's brigade took Mogaung in

00:14:11.755 --> 00:14:15.375
June 1944, after almost a month of

00:14:15.375 --> 00:14:17.655
some of the most savage close-quarters

00:14:17.655 --> 00:14:19.525
fighting of the entire war.

00:14:20.585 --> 00:14:22.525
The cost was appalling.

00:14:23.405 --> 00:14:24.825
He had started the campaign

00:14:25.025 --> 00:14:27.655
with around 3,500 men.

00:14:28.435 --> 00:14:31.085
By the time Mogaung fell, battle,

00:14:31.385 --> 00:14:34.385
disease and sheer exhaustion had

00:14:34.385 --> 00:14:37.247
reduced that number to around 300.

00:14:38.437 --> 00:14:40.315
And then, a few days later,

00:14:40.655 --> 00:14:42.245
Calvert's radio operators

00:14:42.535 --> 00:14:44.775
picked up a BBC news report.

00:14:45.635 --> 00:14:47.555
General Stilwell's headquarters

00:14:47.865 --> 00:14:49.645
had announced that Mogaung had

00:14:49.645 --> 00:14:52.145
been captured by Chinese troops.

00:14:52.505 --> 00:14:53.505
There was no mention

00:14:53.715 --> 00:14:54.888
of the Chindits at all.

00:14:56.185 --> 00:14:58.335
Calvert's reply has gone down

00:14:58.525 --> 00:15:00.245
in British military legend.

00:15:01.185 --> 00:15:03.105
He sent a signal to Stilwell's

00:15:03.115 --> 00:15:05.835
headquarters that read: "Chinese

00:15:05.845 --> 00:15:08.085
reported taking Mogaung. My

00:15:08.085 --> 00:15:10.165
Brigade now taking umbrage."

00:15:11.165 --> 00:15:14.495
To take umbrage, by the way, means to be

00:15:14.505 --> 00:15:16.975
offended, to take insult at something.

00:15:17.845 --> 00:15:19.915
Even then it was an old-fashioned

00:15:19.915 --> 00:15:21.655
and very British expression.

00:15:22.395 --> 00:15:24.745
The story goes, and this part may

00:15:24.745 --> 00:15:26.865
be more legend than fact, that

00:15:26.865 --> 00:15:29.345
Stilwell's American staff searched

00:15:29.355 --> 00:15:31.985
their maps for a town called Umbrage,

00:15:32.145 --> 00:15:33.845
trying to work out where it was.

00:15:35.645 --> 00:15:37.795
Shortly afterwards, when Stilwell's

00:15:37.805 --> 00:15:40.105
headquarters ordered his shattered

00:15:40.105 --> 00:15:42.285
brigade on to yet another battle,

00:15:42.735 --> 00:15:45.835
Calvert shut down his radios, refused,

00:15:46.095 --> 00:15:48.425
and went to see Stilwell in person,

00:15:48.765 --> 00:15:50.895
to give him a piece of his mind.

00:15:52.275 --> 00:15:54.645
When the pair finally met face

00:15:54.645 --> 00:15:57.435
to face, Stilwell understood what

00:15:57.445 --> 00:15:58.675
the brigade had been through.

00:15:59.245 --> 00:16:01.335
The American general relented, and

00:16:01.335 --> 00:16:03.675
the survivors were flown out to India.

00:16:04.615 --> 00:16:07.055
Calvert was awarded the Distinguished

00:16:07.065 --> 00:16:10.045
Service Order for a second time, as

00:16:10.055 --> 00:16:12.515
well as the American Silver Star.

00:16:13.505 --> 00:16:15.365
Three separate commanding officers

00:16:15.595 --> 00:16:17.545
recommended him for the Victoria

00:16:17.545 --> 00:16:20.013
Cross, although he never received it.

00:16:21.073 --> 00:16:24.313
By 1945, Calvert's reputation was

00:16:24.313 --> 00:16:25.823
such that he was given command

00:16:25.843 --> 00:16:28.493
of the SAS Brigade, the special

00:16:28.493 --> 00:16:30.613
forces units fighting in Europe.

00:16:31.393 --> 00:16:34.583
The war ended soon afterwards, and Britain

00:16:34.843 --> 00:16:37.443
began dismantling its special forces.

00:16:38.153 --> 00:16:41.093
The SAS, in the eyes of the military

00:16:41.093 --> 00:16:43.243
establishment, was an expensive

00:16:43.273 --> 00:16:45.593
wartime experiment with no place

00:16:45.613 --> 00:16:48.013
in peacetime, and in October

00:16:48.193 --> 00:16:51.053
1945, it was formally disbanded.

00:16:52.423 --> 00:16:54.723
Calvert thought it was a mistake.

00:16:55.173 --> 00:16:57.563
He pushed hard for an official study

00:16:57.753 --> 00:16:59.863
into what the SAS had achieved and

00:16:59.953 --> 00:17:02.323
what it could do in future wars.

00:17:03.103 --> 00:17:05.233
That study helped keep the idea

00:17:05.233 --> 00:17:08.213
alive, and a part-time territorial

00:17:08.273 --> 00:17:11.333
SAS unit was formed in 1947.

00:17:12.638 --> 00:17:15.893
But the real second birth came

00:17:15.903 --> 00:17:18.163
in Malaya, the then-British

00:17:18.183 --> 00:17:20.373
colony now known as Malaysia.

00:17:21.943 --> 00:17:24.473
In 1948, a communist

00:17:24.513 --> 00:17:26.343
uprising had broken out.

00:17:26.953 --> 00:17:29.333
Communist fighters were operating out of

00:17:29.333 --> 00:17:32.323
the deep jungle, ambushing, killing and

00:17:32.333 --> 00:17:35.383
disappearing, and the British were losing.

00:17:37.033 --> 00:17:40.383
In 1950, Field Marshal Slim, the

00:17:40.573 --> 00:17:42.913
most respected British soldier of his

00:17:42.913 --> 00:17:45.823
generation, described Calvert as perhaps

00:17:45.823 --> 00:17:48.103
the greatest expert on guerrilla warfare

00:17:48.363 --> 00:17:50.013
then serving in the British Army.

00:17:50.843 --> 00:17:53.063
Calvert was asked to study the

00:17:53.063 --> 00:17:55.453
problem and propose a solution.

00:17:56.703 --> 00:17:59.593
He spent months touring Malaya, often

00:17:59.603 --> 00:18:02.422
going out on patrols himself, and he

00:18:02.502 --> 00:18:05.233
produced a report whose central message

00:18:05.393 --> 00:18:08.783
was blunt: there must be one man, with

00:18:08.803 --> 00:18:11.953
one plan, and the power to carry it out.

00:18:13.053 --> 00:18:15.133
One of his recommendations was

00:18:15.133 --> 00:18:17.033
for a special force that could

00:18:17.033 --> 00:18:19.483
live and fight in the deep jungle

00:18:19.533 --> 00:18:22.113
for weeks at a time, beating the

00:18:22.123 --> 00:18:24.263
communist fighters at their own game.

00:18:25.183 --> 00:18:26.983
And naturally, Calvert was

00:18:26.983 --> 00:18:28.443
told to go and create it.

00:18:29.783 --> 00:18:31.920
The unit he formed in 1950

00:18:32.063 --> 00:18:34.153
was called the Malayan Scouts.

00:18:34.913 --> 00:18:36.993
He was given very little support

00:18:37.383 --> 00:18:39.153
and, in many cases, the men

00:18:39.223 --> 00:18:40.633
no other commander wanted.

00:18:41.523 --> 00:18:44.003
He trained them his own way, with

00:18:44.003 --> 00:18:45.863
an emphasis on initiative and

00:18:45.893 --> 00:18:48.493
independence rather than parade-ground

00:18:48.493 --> 00:18:50.532
discipline, and he sent them

00:18:50.583 --> 00:18:52.863
into the jungle in small patrols.

00:18:54.093 --> 00:18:55.683
His training methods were

00:18:56.213 --> 00:18:58.303
unusual, to put it mildly.

00:18:59.043 --> 00:19:01.593
At one point, his men practised jungle

00:19:01.603 --> 00:19:04.773
combat by stalking each other through the

00:19:04.773 --> 00:19:07.723
undergrowth armed with air rifles, wearing

00:19:07.723 --> 00:19:10.073
fencing masks to protect their faces.

00:19:10.873 --> 00:19:13.213
If you got shot, you had been careless,

00:19:13.513 --> 00:19:15.733
and the sting of the pellet was a

00:19:15.763 --> 00:19:17.843
pretty strong incentive not to be.

00:19:18.793 --> 00:19:20.643
The unit was rough around

00:19:20.643 --> 00:19:22.513
the edges, and it picked up a

00:19:22.513 --> 00:19:24.353
reputation for bad behaviour.

00:19:24.809 --> 00:19:27.293
But the core idea worked.

00:19:28.693 --> 00:19:32.033
In 1952, the Malayan Scouts were renamed.

00:19:32.423 --> 00:19:36.183
They became 22 SAS, the special forces

00:19:36.262 --> 00:19:39.023
regiment that still exists to this day.

00:19:40.333 --> 00:19:43.163
Calvert, though, was increasingly ill.

00:19:43.973 --> 00:19:46.723
By the middle of 1951, his

00:19:46.723 --> 00:19:49.053
body had finally given up.

00:19:49.983 --> 00:19:52.853
Malaria, dysentery, miscellaneous

00:19:52.853 --> 00:19:55.103
jungle diseases and twelve years of

00:19:55.103 --> 00:19:57.643
hard soldiering had wrecked his health,

00:19:58.043 --> 00:20:00.033
and he was sent home to recover.

00:20:01.213 --> 00:20:03.073
He was 38 years old.

00:20:03.323 --> 00:20:05.693
He had every reason to expect that this

00:20:05.693 --> 00:20:08.633
was a temporary blip, and that the best

00:20:08.633 --> 00:20:10.843
part of his career was still ahead of him.

00:20:12.003 --> 00:20:13.803
Unfortunately, it was not.

00:20:14.923 --> 00:20:19.063
In 1951, Calvert was posted to Germany, to

00:20:19.063 --> 00:20:21.693
the British forces stationed near Hanover.

00:20:22.423 --> 00:20:24.993
And it was there, within months,

00:20:25.163 --> 00:20:26.903
that his world collapsed.

00:20:27.743 --> 00:20:29.463
He was accused of gross

00:20:29.496 --> 00:20:32.013
indecency with young German men.

00:20:32.763 --> 00:20:35.483
In other words, homosexual acts, which

00:20:35.643 --> 00:20:38.003
at the time were a criminal offence

00:20:38.233 --> 00:20:40.513
under both British and military law.

00:20:41.393 --> 00:20:42.873
He was court-martialled,

00:20:43.253 --> 00:20:45.063
tried in a military court.

00:20:46.163 --> 00:20:48.383
The evidence against him was thin

00:20:48.663 --> 00:20:50.923
and contradictory, and part of

00:20:50.923 --> 00:20:53.233
it was later withdrawn by the

00:20:53.233 --> 00:20:54.973
very witnesses who had given it.

00:20:55.843 --> 00:20:57.173
He was found guilty anyway.

00:20:57.663 --> 00:21:00.833
The sentence was dismissal from the army.

00:21:02.313 --> 00:21:04.073
For a man like Calvert,

00:21:04.403 --> 00:21:06.393
this was a form of death.

00:21:07.563 --> 00:21:09.513
The army had been his entire

00:21:09.513 --> 00:21:11.663
life since the age of 19.

00:21:12.773 --> 00:21:14.933
In a single day, he lost his

00:21:14.933 --> 00:21:17.013
career, his reputation, his

00:21:17.213 --> 00:21:19.653
income and his place in the world.

00:21:20.473 --> 00:21:23.533
The hero of Mogaung was now, in the eyes

00:21:23.533 --> 00:21:26.513
of society, a criminal and a disgrace.

00:21:27.483 --> 00:21:28.683
He denied the charge

00:21:28.833 --> 00:21:30.473
until the day he died.

00:21:31.073 --> 00:21:33.643
His biographer, who spent years

00:21:33.683 --> 00:21:35.873
studying the case, came to believe

00:21:36.103 --> 00:21:37.773
he had been framed, that is,

00:21:38.003 --> 00:21:40.433
deliberately set up with false evidence.

00:21:41.413 --> 00:21:44.183
Calvert had made enemies in his career,

00:21:44.363 --> 00:21:46.783
so perhaps this was their revenge.

00:21:47.303 --> 00:21:48.933
Or perhaps it was something

00:21:48.933 --> 00:21:50.623
closer to a robbery gone wrong.

00:21:51.893 --> 00:21:53.503
The young men in question later

00:21:53.533 --> 00:21:54.903
admitted that they had gone

00:21:54.903 --> 00:21:56.763
to his flat to steal from it.

00:21:57.723 --> 00:21:59.923
The court martial records were sealed

00:22:00.263 --> 00:22:02.043
and remain closed to the public

00:22:02.133 --> 00:22:06.033
until 2028, so even today, no one can

00:22:06.033 --> 00:22:07.903
read the full evidence against him.

00:22:09.113 --> 00:22:12.503
What is certain is what happened next.

00:22:13.263 --> 00:22:14.693
Calvert drifted.

00:22:14.783 --> 00:22:16.893
He drank, heavily, and

00:22:16.893 --> 00:22:17.953
became an alcoholic.

00:22:18.483 --> 00:22:20.523
He tried to build a new life as an

00:22:20.553 --> 00:22:24.163
engineer in Australia, and it fell apart.

00:22:25.153 --> 00:22:28.033
At his lowest point, this man who

00:22:28.033 --> 00:22:29.913
had commanded thousands in battle

00:22:30.233 --> 00:22:32.463
was doing casual manual labour,

00:22:32.793 --> 00:22:35.313
sleeping rough, an anonymous drifter

00:22:35.473 --> 00:22:36.913
on the other side of the world.

00:22:38.393 --> 00:22:40.033
Slowly, he managed to

00:22:40.113 --> 00:22:41.613
claw some of it back.

00:22:42.273 --> 00:22:44.173
He wrote well-regarded books

00:22:44.303 --> 00:22:45.753
about the Burma campaign.

00:22:45.943 --> 00:22:48.103
He lectured on guerrilla warfare,

00:22:48.373 --> 00:22:51.383
and in the 1970s he appeared as an

00:22:51.433 --> 00:22:53.643
expert in the celebrated television

00:22:53.653 --> 00:22:56.093
documentary series The World at War.

00:22:57.013 --> 00:22:59.263
Old comrades, especially SAS

00:22:59.263 --> 00:23:02.213
men, stood by him and campaigned

00:23:02.403 --> 00:23:03.983
to restore his reputation.

00:23:05.113 --> 00:23:08.053
But the stain of his 1952

00:23:08.053 --> 00:23:09.953
court martial never left him.

00:23:10.843 --> 00:23:13.323
In his lifetime, there was no

00:23:13.323 --> 00:23:16.553
pardon, no review, no apology.

00:23:17.303 --> 00:23:19.153
He never married, he struggled with

00:23:19.163 --> 00:23:22.563
money all his life, and in 1997

00:23:22.803 --> 00:23:25.003
he was forced to sell his medals.

00:23:26.043 --> 00:23:28.003
He died the following year, in

00:23:28.003 --> 00:23:31.203
November 1998, at the age of 85.

00:23:32.313 --> 00:23:34.533
Today, the regiment he rebuilt

00:23:34.783 --> 00:23:37.833
is one of if not the most famous

00:23:37.843 --> 00:23:40.013
special forces unit in the world.

00:23:40.633 --> 00:23:42.843
The tactics he worked out in the jungles

00:23:42.853 --> 00:23:45.553
of Burma and Malaya became standard

00:23:45.553 --> 00:23:47.703
practice, taught to generations of

00:23:47.703 --> 00:23:49.873
soldiers who have never heard his name.

00:23:50.953 --> 00:23:53.983
And in 2028, the sealed files

00:23:54.023 --> 00:23:55.753
from that court martial in Germany

00:23:56.053 --> 00:23:58.013
are due to be opened at last.

00:23:58.813 --> 00:24:01.833
Thirty years after his death, "Mad Mike"

00:24:01.833 --> 00:24:04.716
Calvert may finally get his answer.

00:24:06.093 --> 00:24:08.403
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:24:08.403 --> 00:24:10.643
episode on "Mad Mike" Calvert.

00:24:10.863 --> 00:24:12.163
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:24:12.453 --> 00:24:14.343
and that you've learnt something new.

00:24:14.943 --> 00:24:16.833
You've been listening to English Learning

00:24:16.833 --> 00:24:19.353
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:19.683 --> 00:24:22.463
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:22.493 --> 00:24:25.033
I'll catch you in the next episode.

